{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for exact clean head f9af5400df01607ad716286c12a593d7ae4547c5. Four findings. (1) The tier-1 trigger and key boundary do not compose. The proposed inbox commit's tree is exactly the payload tree, so it contains no .github/workflows file for a push workflow to run. If the branch instead carries the workflow, GitHub documents that push workflows can run from commits not merged to the default branch and that write users can create/modify workflow files and harvest referenced secrets or the write GITHUB_TOKEN. Therefore 'one workflow and one secret', 'record authority is unchanged', and 'a writer costs availability only' are false unless the privileged sequencer workflow is isolated from inbox writers. Specify that control plane and add a malicious-workflow/secret-exfiltration spike case; GitHub's new workflow-execution protections are public preview, not an implicit invariant. Evidence: https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/events-that-trigger-workflows and https://docs.github.com/en/actions/concepts/security/compromised-runners. (2) refs/heads/inbox/\u003cfingerprint\u003e/\u003cidempotency-key\u003e is not the kernel dedup identity. The authoritative identity is target + actor + namespace + key; one repo can contain many logs, so honest concurrent submissions reusing a key across targets or namespaces collide. The intent accepts any nonempty key without CR/LF/NUL, while many such valid keys (for example one containing a space) are invalid refnames. Use a ref-safe digest/encoding of the full dedup tuple or a separately verified intake identifier, and test cross-log, cross-namespace, and hostile-valid-key cases. (3) The refusal protocol contradicts 'silent disappearance is never an outcome'. A marker deleted after TTL leaves a late-polling submitter with no log event, marker, or inbox branch; and the note neither requires an atomic sequence/marker + inbox-delete ref transaction nor tests crashes between those updates. Define acknowledgement/retention and atomic or recoverable transitions, then add late-poll and crash-window cases. (4) R8-R9 overstate verification. A normal git clone does not fetch refs/seq/* (the bootstrap and gs attach implementation say so), so 'backup is git clone', 'key plus any clone', and 'any clone verifies' need an attached/configured clone. A published but unretained sequencer-signed checkpoint does not bound a misbehaving host or equivocation; it protects a reader that retains and verifies it, while witnesses close the cross-reader gap. State those holders and guarantees explicitly. What passes: the default concurrency-group coalescing claim is accurate and justifies a drain loop; the availability correction for forge-primary refs is warranted outside the tier-1 secret issue; R1 correctly reuses the ratified capability-token design; R2-R7 and R10 are coherent requirements; the exact commit trailer resolves to request 249f97da. make vet/test/build and diff --check pass; only the note changed, UI sources/assets are untouched, and the worktree is clean. No merge performed.","body":{"finding_count":"4","head":"f9af5400df01607ad716286c12a593d7ae4547c5","verdict":"changes-requested"}}